Our support team forwarded a customer report that says the export button in the reporting dashboard has been returning an empty file since Tuesday morning. The dashboard runs in a staging deployment, and I have shell access to the staging host. I have already looked at the web server's access log and confirmed that the export endpoint is being called and that it answers with HTTP 200, so the failure is happening inside the export handler rather than at the routing layer. The handler writes a temporary file under /var/tmp/report-export and then streams it back to the browser, and the file that reaches the customer is always zero bytes. That points at the step that populates the temporary file, but there are several plausible causes: a disk-space problem on the staging volume, a permissions problem on the directory the worker process writes into, or an exception that the handler swallows while leaving the file empty. Before I start reading application code I want to know whether the host itself is healthy with respect to the filesystem. The most direct evidence would be the amount of free space on each mounted filesystem and the permissions of the export directory. I could log in interactively, but a single diagnostic command run on the host will answer several questions at once: it can report disk usage and list the directory with its ownership and mode bits. I do not want to restart any services, I do not want to delete temporary files yet, and I do not want to change permissions before I understand what is wrong. The command should be safe to run on a production-adjacent host, should complete quickly, and should need no interactive input. Give it a reasonable timeout so that a stalled mount cannot hang the session. Assume the host is reachable as the staging machine named in the incident and that I am allowed to run read-only diagnostics there. The command I have in mind is something that prints filesystem usage and then lists the export directory, for example a combined df and ls invocation. Once I see that output I can decide whether to look at the application logs or at disk capacity. The next step is therefore to run that inspection command, not to modify anything. Return the single tool call that performs the next step
